        "In 3.0 NOAID_WIND had three bits: the operator command, learning the wind from fixes, and the Pitot scale and heading corrections. The wind that the filter estimated during a stretch with GPS did not pass into the dead reckoning when the GPS was gone.",
        "NOAID_WIND is the sum of the values of the bits that are on: for example 15 = 1 + 2 + 4 + 8. The value 0 switches the wind off and the dead reckoning assumes calm air.\n• Bit 0, value 1, “operator command”: the firmware accepts the wind entered with a station command or in the Wind card of the configurator and adds it to the dead reckoning; the wind 0 command erases it. For a wind known beforehand (a weather report, an anemometer).\n• Bit 1, value 2, “learning from fixes”: every position fix shows how far the dead reckoning has drifted, and the firmware finds the wind that was missing. For an unknown wind with an operator who can mark the position; place the fixes accurately.\n• Bit 2, value 4, “Pitot scale and heading”: the same fixes teach, together with the wind, the scale error of the airspeed sensor and a heading bias, so that they are not taken for wind; works only together with bit 1.\n• Bit 3, value 8, new, “carry the GPS wind over”: when the GPS is lost in flight, the wind estimated with the GPS goes into the dead reckoning, so nothing has to start from scratch after a stretch with GPS.\n• Bit 4, value 16, new, “always remember”: works only with bit 3 and learning; the GPS wind replaces the own one even when they hardly differ.\nReady values: 1 — command only; 3 — command and learning; 7 — the same with the scale and heading corrections; 15 — like 7 plus the GPS wind carried over (recommended for those who use both fixes and stretches with GPS); 31 — like 15 but the GPS wind is always remembered; 9 — command and carry-over without learning; 8 — carry-over only.\nValues without bit 3 (0 to 7) behave as before: the new bits are off by default."
